Normal Inheritance & Overriding: Use when classes share common behavior that can be inherited and when specific behavior needs to be modified.
Abstract Classes: Use when you have a base class that provides some shared implementation but also has methods that must be implemented by subclasses. It’s suitable for situations where you want to provide some common code across related classes but still enforce certain methods to be implemented.
Interfaces: Use when you need to define a contract that can be implemented by any class, regardless of its position in the class hierarchy. It’s ideal for defining capabilities or behaviors that might be shared across completely unrelated classes.

